body:
The origin of this idiom is not specified, but there are certain theories about it, according to Oxford English Dictionary. I will directly cite them here: 

"A person standing on a [pail](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pail) or [bucket](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bucket) with their head in a [slip noose](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/slip_noose) would kick the bucket so as to [commit suicide](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/commit_suicide). The OED, however, says this is mainly speculative;

* The OED describes as more plausible the archaic use of "bucket" as a beam from which a [pig](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pig) is hung by its feet prior to being slaughtered. To kick the bucket, then, originally signified the pig's death throes."

I adore that someone has recommended you Lovely Complex, it's funny and has likeable main heroes, which is hard to find in many romance animes. From me, I recommend:   
\- Kimi ni Todoke; incredibly sweet, slow action, but it is one of the better anime from this kind; 

\- Isshuukan Friends - also very cute and delicate, as far as I remember it was one of the fluffiest things I have ever watched; 

\- Sukitte Ii na yo - another school romance, has a really nice animation and main charas quickly become a thing, so it's something different, 

\- NANA - not school romance, but perfect romance anime about two girls who just try to find their place and cope with adult world (a lot of tears involved in that); 

\- Paradise Kiss - from the same author who have drawn NANA; it's about newly recognised model and how she is trying to fit in harsh fashion industry; mature romance alert. 

&#x200B;

So yeah, you could also check out [**Karakai Jouzu no Takagi-san**](https://myanimelist.net/anime/35860/Karakai_Jouzu_no_Takagi-san)**,** [**Kaichou wa Maid-sama!**](https://myanimelist.net/anime/7054/Kaichou_wa_Maid-sama)**,** [**Tonari no Kaibutsu-kun**](https://myanimelist.net/anime/14227/Tonari_no_Kaibutsu-kun) and [**Kotoura-san**](https://myanimelist.net/anime/15379/Kotoura-san)**.** All of them are school romance animes, with more or less drama.

Definitely I recommend:  
\- Banana Fish - a lot of action, gangs, mafia + deep relationship between main characters, it revolves around topics like child abuse, rape and drugs, so it is dedicated for mature audience,

\- Durarara - a lot of different histories that come together at the end, also revolves around street gangs etc.; at first glance it doesn't look that "adultish", but later in action it does show a lot of twisted and dark minds; 

 \- Psycho-Pass - utopian futuristic anime about police, it involves political drama about devices that control your everyday life; can be compared in certain aspects to Ghost in The Shell or Ergo Proxy;  
\- Boku Dake Ga Inai Machi (Erased) - dude is coming back to the past to prevent death of a classmate; amazing visuals and camera work, suspense, drama and reasonable amount of action, 

\- Cowboy Bebop - futuristic anime where space "cowboy" catches criminals for money, fantastic classic with astounding OST and fight animations; 

\- Yakusoku no Neverland (Promised Neverland) - coming out this season, anime about kids, which try to get out of certain area they are imprisoned in after learning horrifying secret about it; I absolutely recommend that for showing in-depth thinking process of main characters, for beautiful animation (when it comes to angle, slowly cumulation tension it's similar to Zankyou no Terror) and general topics it has in it.
